<Prose title="Privacy Policy" description="LanDeck doesn't collect, store or share any personal data. What it learns about your network stays on your Mac." updated="6 November 2026">
  <p class="intro">
    <strong>LanDeck does not collect, store or share any personal data.</strong> What it learns about your network stays
    on your Mac. This applies to both editions: the Mac App Store version and the direct download.
  </p>

  <h2>What LanDeck does on your network</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It looks, and only looks.</strong> To find the devices on the network your Mac is connected to, LanDeck sends
      small requests to them: it pings them, reads the Mac’s own address table, listens for Bonjour and UPnP announcements,
      asks for names (reverse DNS, NetBIOS, SNMP), checks whether common ports are open by connecting and closing at once,
      and reads the certificate of a device’s secure web page without trusting it or sending anything. It never signs in to
      a device, never changes one and never opens a port on your router.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Router port forwards.</strong> If your router lets apps open ports to the internet (UPnP), LanDeck asks the
      router, and only the router, which ports are open. It only reads that list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stays on your network.</strong> All of this happens between your Mac and the devices on your network. None of
      it is sent anywhere else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What stays on your Mac</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Devices and your changes.</strong> The devices LanDeck has found on each network, with the names, types,
      notes, tags, favourites and watchlist you set, are kept in LanDeck’s Application Support folder on your Mac.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>History (LanDeck Pro).</strong> When each device was online and how fast it answered, and changes such as new
      devices and new open ports, are kept on your Mac for 30, 60 or 90 days, as you choose in Settings. Clear History
      deletes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Speed tests and the outage log.</strong> Your speed test results, and the outage log (when the internet
      was down, for how long and why), are kept on your Mac with the history. Clear History deletes the outage log; Clear
      History under Speed deletes the speed tests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Guest Wi‑Fi code (LanDeck Pro).</strong> If you make a QR code for your guest Wi‑Fi, the network name and
      password you type are kept on your Mac: the password in your Keychain. LanDeck never reads Wi‑Fi passwords from
      macOS.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Settings</strong> are kept in LanDeck’s preferences on your Mac.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Device makers</strong> are looked up in a list that comes with the app, not online.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications</strong> (new devices, watched devices, security, the weekly summary) are made on your Mac and
      shown by macOS.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When LanDeck goes online</h2>
  <p>LanDeck only connects to something outside your network when you ask it to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Internet check.</strong> When you open Internet in the sidebar, click Check Now, or open the menu bar
      panel (Settings › General can turn that off), LanDeck checks whether your internet works. It opens a connection to
      Cloudflare (<code>1.1.1.1</code>) and Google (<code>8.8.8.8</code>) and closes it at once, loads Apple’s test page
      (<code>captive.apple.com</code>), the one Macs use to spot Wi‑Fi sign-in pages, and asks your network’s DNS server
      for <code>apple.com</code>. Those services see these requests as they would any website visit; nothing about your
      network or its devices is sent. It doesn’t check in the background unless you turn on the outage log (below).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Speed test.</strong> When you click Test Speed, LanDeck runs macOS’s own speed test tool
      (<code>networkQuality</code>), which downloads and uploads test data with Apple’s servers: from about 100 MB to
      over 1 GB, depending on your connection’s speed. See
      <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple’s privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Outage log (LanDeck Pro, off unless you turn it on).</strong> While it’s on and LanDeck is open, LanDeck
      checks once a minute that the internet answers: it opens and closes a connection to Cloudflare
      (<code>1.1.1.1</code>) and Google (<code>8.8.8.8</code>) and asks your network’s DNS server for
      <code>apple.com</code>. When one fails, it runs the internet check above to find out why. Nothing else is sent.
      Turn it off in Settings › Monitoring.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your public address.</strong> In Tools › Connection, Look Up asks Cloudflare
      (<code>1.1.1.1/cdn-cgi/trace</code>) for the internet address your network uses, and its country. Cloudflare sees
      that request, as it would any website visit; see
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tools you run.</strong> Ping, Traceroute, Port Scan and DNS Lookup contact the address or DNS server you type
      or choose, and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Purchases.</strong> LanDeck Pro is bought and managed through the App Store. Apple handles the payment;
      LanDeck only learns whether your subscription is active.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Permissions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local Network:</strong> to find and talk to the devices on your network.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Location (optional):</strong> macOS only shares your Wi‑Fi network’s name with apps that have Location
      access. LanDeck uses it only to show that name. It never reads, stores or sends your location.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Notifications (optional):</strong> for LanDeck Pro’s alerts and weekly summary.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>No tracking</h2>
  <p>LanDeck has no analytics, no advertising and no account. It doesn’t identify you or follow what you do.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website has no cookies, analytics or tracking. It remembers whether you chose dark or light screenshots in your
    browser’s storage, on your device. It’s hosted by GitHub Pages, which may log visitors’ IP addresses for security, as
    described in
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s privacy statement</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be here with a new date. Questions are welcome on the
    <a href={site.supportUrl}>LanDeck support page on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
